---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { withBase } from '@lib/paths';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = withBase(`/tool/${category.segment}/${config.slug}/`);
const findReplaceHref = withBase('/tool/text/find-replace/');
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>

  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>Quick Answer <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      A regex tester (also called a regular expression tester or online regex tool) lets you try a
      pattern against sample text and see every match before you ship it. This one uses your
      browser's JavaScript <code>RegExp</code> engine, lists match indices and capture groups, and
      can preview a replace with <code>$1</code> or named groups.
    </p>
    <p>
      When you need to test regex online, paste a pattern, add regex flags, and drop in a
      representative string. Matches update live. Expensive shapes are refused, and any run that
      takes too long is stopped so the tab cannot hang.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Try the Regex Tester →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="what-is-a-regex-tester">
    <h2>What Is a Live Regex Matcher? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      A regular expression is a compact way to describe a set of strings: digits, email-shaped
      tokens, log lines, or the word boundaries around a product name. Writing one by eye is easy.
      Trusting it is harder, because a pattern that looks right can still miss edge cases or capture
      the wrong slice.
    </p>
    <p>
      A regex tester is the scratchpad for that trust. You feed it a pattern and some text, then read
      what actually matched. Indices tell you where. Groups tell you what each pair of parentheses
      held. A replace preview shows the edited string without touching your real document yet.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="Match count is not understanding">
      <p>
        Three matches can still mean three empty optional groups. Read the group list under each
        hit before you decide the pattern is done.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="how-to-test">
    <h2>How to Test a Regular Expression Online <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Start with the smallest text that shows the behaviour you care about. A single log line beats
      a 10,000-line dump when you are still shaping the pattern. Type the expression between the
      slashes, set flags, and watch the match list.
    </p>
    <p>
      Use <code>g</code> when you expect multiple hits (the tester adds it when missing so every
      match can be listed). Use <code>i</code> for case-insensitive matching, <code>m</code> so
      <code>^</code> and <code>$</code> apply per line, and <code>s</code> so <code>.</code> can
      cross newlines. The <code>d</code> flag asks the engine for match indices when you need exact
      spans.
    </p>
    <p>
      Example: pattern <code>(?&lt;word&gt;\b\w&#123;4,&#125;\b)</code> with flags <code>g</code> against
      "The quick brown fox" reports each word of four or more letters, names the group
      <code>word</code>, and can rewrite matches as <code>[$&lt;word&gt;]</code> in the replace
      field.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="capture-groups">
    <h2>How Capture Groups Work <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Parentheses do two jobs. They group parts of the pattern for quantifiers, and they capture the
      text that matched that part. Numbered groups are <code>$1</code>, <code>$2</code>, and so on
      in a replacement. Named groups use <code>(?&lt;name&gt;...)</code> in the pattern and
      <code>$&lt;name&gt;</code> in the replacement.
    </p>
    <p>
      Optional groups that did not participate show as <code>undefined</code> in the match explain
      list. That is the usual reason a replace looks fine while a downstream parser still breaks:
      the group you expected was empty on some hits. Reading the regex match groups under each hit
      is therefore the point of the tool, not an extra detail.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="javascript-flavour">
    <h2>Which Regex Flavour Is This? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      This tester is JavaScript <code>RegExp</code>, not PCRE, not Python's <code>re</code>, and not
      the Rust regex crate. Most everyday patterns transfer. Lookbehind, named groups, and Unicode
      property escapes depend on browser support. Constructs that exist only in PCRE (for example
      recursive patterns) will not compile here, and that is intentional: the goal is to preview
      what your front-end or Node code will actually do.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Flavour mismatches waste an afternoon">
      <p>
        A pattern copied from a PCRE cheat sheet can fail with a syntax error in JavaScript, or
        worse, compile but match differently. When the target runtime is JavaScript, test in
        JavaScript.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="expensive-patterns">
    <h2>Why Some Patterns Are Blocked <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Nested quantifiers such as <code>(a+)+</code> and overlapping alternatives such as
      <code>(a|ab)+</code> can force the matcher to explore an exponential number of paths. On the
      wrong input that freezes the tab. The tester refuses those shapes up front and also kills any
      run that exceeds a short time budget.
    </p>
    <p>
      Test text is capped near 20,000 characters for the same reason. If you need to validate a huge
      corpus, take a representative slice first, then move the proven pattern into your pipeline.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="tester-vs-find-replace">
    <h2>Regex Tester vs Find and Replace <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Use this tester when the question is "what does this pattern do?" Use
      <a href={findReplaceHref}>Find and Replace</a> when the question is "change every match in
      this document." The replace field here is a preview for learning and debugging. Find and
      Replace is the editor workflow, with match highlighting across the source.
    </p>
    <p>
      A practical loop: shape the pattern here until groups look right, preview the replace, then
      paste the same pattern into Find and Replace (with the regex toggle on) to edit the real text.
      Diff the before and after with Text Compare if you want a second check.
    </p>
  </section>


  <section id="common-mistakes">
    <h2>Common Mistakes <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Treating a green match count as proof. Three hits can still mean three empty optional groups.
      Always open the group list under a match before you trust the pattern.
    </p>
    <p>
      Pasting a PCRE-only construct into a JavaScript regexp tester. Recursive patterns and some
      lookaround forms that work in PHP will throw a syntax error here, or worse, compile and match
      differently. Therefore, test in the same flavour you will ship.
    </p>
    <p>
      Shipping nested quantifiers such as <code>(a+)+</code> because they "looked fine" on short
      happy-path input. On crafted text they hang. Prefer tighter classes, and keep the expensive
      shapes out of production even if a different tool lets them run.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Empty groups hide inside a happy count">
      <p>
        For example, <code>(\d+)?-(\w+)</code> against "abc" can still report a match while group 1
        is undefined. A regex replace preview that inserts <code>$1</code> then silently drops the
        digits you thought were required.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="privacy">
    <h2>Is My Text Uploaded? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      No. Matching runs entirely in your browser with JavaScript. Patterns, flags, and test text
      never leave the device. That matters when the sample is a production log line, a customer
      record, or an auth token shaped string you should not paste into a stranger's server.
    </p>
  </section>

</GuideLayout>
